dictionary/**/data/cleaned/drop.csv

# === Rust ===
/engine/target/
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock.bak

# === Rust shared-core platform artifacts ===
# `make build` output: the iOS and macOS xcframeworks with their swift-bridge
# wrappers, and the Android .so per ABI. These were committed under D9.2 so a
# release tag carried a complete, buildable engine — reversed 2026-09-07 in
# favour of a tag plus a reproducible `make build`, because the .a and .so were
# rewritten into history on every engine change and accounted for most of the
# repository's size.
#
# The stable paths themselves stay: ios/RustEngine/ and macos/RustEngine/ hold
# the hand-written README and SwiftLoggerSink.swift, and Xcode / SwiftPM
# reference the framework at a fixed location under them.
ios/RustEngine/RustTaigi.xcframework/
ios/RustEngine/RustTaigi.swift
ios/RustEngine/SwiftBridgeCore.swift
macos/RustEngine/RustTaigi.xcframework/
macos/RustEngine/RustTaigi.swift
macos/RustEngine/SwiftBridgeCore.swift
android/app/src/main/jniLibs/**/*.so

# === Font copies (scripts/sync-fonts.sh) ===
# `ios/Resources/Fonts` is the committed source. macOS and Windows keep their
# own copies because their packaging scripts read from their own platform
# directory, but those copies are `make fonts` output, not input — 79 MB of
# duplicate bytes that changed in git every time a face was added or replaced.
# Run `make fonts` after cloning; `make fonts-check` verifies they match.
# Android is NOT here: its res/font/ copies use Android resource naming and two
# of the four differ from the source in three bytes of `head` checksum, so
# sync-fonts.sh deliberately does not manage them (see its header).
macos/Resources/Fonts/
windows/resources/Fonts/

# === SwiftPM build cache (macOS input method) ===
# `.build/` holds compiled products, the assembled `.app` from
# macos/scripts/bundle-app.sh, and the signed installer packages from
# macos/scripts/release-app.sh; `Package.resolved` is committed.
macos/.build/
macos/.swiftpm/

# === Xcode build cache ===
# DerivedData lives outside the repo by default, but xcodebuild with
# `-derivedDataPath build/` (or a script that does) drops XCBuildData here.
ios/build/

# === Credentials ===
# Nothing of this shape has ever been committed (verified over the full history,
# 2026-09-05) and nothing should be. Ignoring the shapes keeps an accidental
# `git add .` from staging one; .githooks/pre-commit and the Secrets workflow
# catch what gets past this.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.mobileprovision
*.cer
credentials.json
client_secret*.json
secrets.properties
keystore.properties

# === Claude Code ===
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/projects/
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# top-level cloned IME repos only — anchored so it does NOT match docs/references/ or spec/references
/references/
.tmp/
# === Windows input method (windows/ Cargo workspace) ===
windows/target/
# `.build/` holds the staged install layout and the installer that
# windows/scripts/release-app.sh produces — the macOS side's `macos/.build/`,
# and it must be ignored for the same reason: the script refuses a dirty tree,
# so its own output would make the next release un-cuttable.
windows/.build/
